sections.body:
`java.util` is built around three collection interfaces, and the choice between them should follow the shape of your data, not habit. A **`List`** is an **ordered**, index-accessible sequence that **permits duplicates**: elements have a definite position (`0`, `1`, `2`, ...), and you can ask for the element at index 2. A **`Set`** guarantees **no duplicate elements** (adding an element that is already present is a no-op, and `add` returns `false` rather than throwing) and, depending on the implementation, may or may not keep any particular order. A **`Map`** stores **key-to-value associations**, where every key maps to one value and lookup by key is the whole purpose. There is no index in the List sense, only keys.

```java
List<String> tags = new ArrayList<>();
tags.add("java");
tags.add("java");         // fine, duplicates allowed
System.out.println(tags.size());   // 2

Set<String> uniqueTags = new HashSet<>();
uniqueTags.add("java");
uniqueTags.add("java");   // ignored, already present
System.out.println(uniqueTags.size());   // 1

Map<String, Integer> wordCounts = new HashMap<>();
wordCounts.put("java", 1);
wordCounts.put("java", 2);   // OVERWRITES the previous value for this key
System.out.println(wordCounts.get("java"));   // 2
```

Notice that the declared type on the left of each declaration is the **interface** (`List`, `Set`, `Map`), not the class (`ArrayList`, `HashSet`, `HashMap`) constructed on the right. This is idiomatic Java, not decoration. Code written against the interface type is checked against the interface's contract only, so you can swap the implementation later (from `ArrayList` to `LinkedList`, say, once you find you need fast insertion at the front) by changing just the `new` expression. Every other line that only called `List` methods on `tags` keeps compiling and working.

`HashSet` and `HashMap` (the most commonly used `Set` and `Map` implementations) rely on **hashing** for fast lookup. An object's `hashCode()` decides which internal bucket it is stored in, and `equals()` then tells apart different objects that land in the same bucket. For this to work, a class must obey one rule: **if two objects are `.equals()` to each other, they must return the same `hashCode()`.** The default `Object.hashCode()` and `Object.equals()` are based on object identity, which is consistent with itself but rarely what you want for a value-like class such as `Point`, where two separately constructed objects with the same data should count as the same element in a `Set` or `Map`.

```java
public class Point {
    private int x, y;

    public Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (!(o instanceof Point)) return false;
        Point p = (Point) o;
        return x == p.x && y == p.y;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(x, y);
    }
}

Set<Point> points = new HashSet<>();
points.add(new Point(1, 2));
System.out.println(points.contains(new Point(1, 2)));   // true, only because equals and hashCode are overridden
```

Without those overrides, `points.contains(new Point(1, 2))` would return `false` right after adding what looks like the same point, because identity-based `equals()` treats the new `Point` as a different object from the stored one, even though `x` and `y` match. Override both methods *together*. If you override only `equals()` and keep the inherited identity-based `hashCode()`, two `.equals()` objects can land in different buckets, and a `HashSet` or `HashMap` will fail to find an entry that `.equals()` says is present.

Hash-based storage has a dangerous consequence. If you mutate an object *after* using it as a `HashSet` element or `HashMap` key, and the mutated field feeds into its `hashCode()`, the hash code changes but the entry does not move to a new bucket. The entry is still in the bucket its *old* hash code pointed to, while every later lookup computes the *new* hash code and searches a different bucket, so it is never found.

```java
List<Integer> tag = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1));
Map<List<Integer>, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(tag, "first item");

tag.add(2);   // mutates the key after insertion, so tag's hashCode changes

System.out.println(map.get(tag));         // null here: looked up under the new hash, stored under the old one
System.out.println(map.containsKey(tag)); // false, for the same reason
```

So use **immutable** objects as `HashSet` elements and `HashMap` keys whenever you can. `String`, `Integer`, and the other wrapper types are safe because nothing can change their hash code after the fact. If a mutable type must be a key, the rule is never to mutate it while it is a live key, which is easy to break by accident once the same object is referenced from several places.

