A class can declare one or more **type parameters** (conventionally single uppercase letters like `T`, `E`, `K`, `V`) in angle brackets after its name, and use that type parameter anywhere a type would otherwise appear inside the class:

```java
public class Box<T> {
    private T contents;

    public void set(T contents) {
        this.contents = contents;
    }

    public T get() {
        return contents;
    }
}

Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>();
stringBox.set("hello");
String value = stringBox.get();   // no cast needed -- the compiler already knows get() returns a String here
```

To a caller, `Box<String>` and `Box<Integer>` behave like two specialized versions of `Box`, one that accepts and returns only `String`, one only `Integer`. The compiler enforces that at every call site and removes the need for a cast on `get()`.

Methods can be generic independently of their class: `public static <T> T firstOf(List<T> list) { return list.get(0); }` declares its own type parameter `T`, inferred from the list passed at each call site. A **bounded type parameter**, `<T extends Comparable<T>>`, restricts `T` to types that implement `Comparable<T>`. That is how a generic method such as a max-finder or sort routine can call `.compareTo()` on its elements and still work for any type that supports comparison.

Two restrictions that look arbitrary make sense once you know about erasure. First, you cannot write `new T[10]` inside a generic class. Creating an array requires the JVM to know the array's element type at run time (arrays, unlike generic collections, keep it for array-store checking), but after erasure `T` is not a type, so there is nothing to allocate an array *of*. The standard workaround is to create an `Object[]` and cast, which is what `ArrayList`'s internal storage does.

Second, you cannot write `if (obj instanceof List<String>)` when `obj` is, say, an `Object`. `instanceof` checks a run-time type, and the "list of `String`s" distinction does not exist at run time. The compiler rejects the check (since Java 16 it accepts a parameterized type only when it can prove the cast is safe); the check you can write is `obj instanceof List`, which asks only whether it is a `List` of something.

```java
public class Box<T> {
    // private T[] items = new T[10];   // compile error -- cannot create a generic array
    private Object[] items = new Object[10];   // the standard erasure workaround

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public T get(int i) {
        return (T) items[i];   // compiler-inserted-style cast, marked unchecked since it can't verify T at run time
    }
}
```

